export const LESSONS={
probability:{title:'Count the event. Name the denominator.',objective:'CH4-01 · Calculate an observed event frequency and its complement; distinguish evidence from a future probability.',body:`<p>A probability describes how likely an event is in a stated model. An <strong>observed relative frequency</strong> is what happened in a specified sample: event count divided by observation count. It lies from 0 to 1; multiply by 100 to express it as a percentage.</p><div class="formula">Observed frequency = event count ÷ sample size<br>Complement frequency = 1 − event frequency</div><p>For A’s six hypothetical monthly returns, the event “return &lt; 0” occurs twice: −3 and −1. Its observed frequency is 2/6 ≈ 0.3333 (33.33%). The complement “return ≥ 0” occurs four times, including the zero month: 4/6 ≈ 0.6667. Together they exhaust the sample.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> if the zero month were called a loss, would the event definition still be “strictly negative”? No. Changing a boundary changes the question. A zero return is not evidence of a gain either.</p><p>Two losses in six invented observations do not establish a one-in-three future loss probability. Sampling uncertainty, changing market conditions and dependence between months can undermine that interpretation. These are original teaching data, not historical investment performance.</p>`,reflection:'Explain which observations you counted, why zero belongs to the complement, and why the observed fraction is not a reliable future promise.',criteria:'I stated the event boundary, denominator, and a limit on extrapolation.'},
centre:{title:'Two ways to describe the centre.',objective:'CH4-02 · Compute an arithmetic mean and median, and explain sensitivity to an extreme observation.',body:`<p>The arithmetic <strong>mean</strong> adds the observations and divides by their count. The <strong>median</strong> sorts the observations and finds the middle; for an even count, average the two middle values. They answer different questions.</p><div class="formula">Mean = Σx / n<br>Median of A = (0 + 1) / 2 = 0.5%</div><p>Worked example: A = [−3, −1, 0, 1, 2, 7]%. The sum is 6%, so its arithmetic mean is 1%. The median is 0.5%. Replacing the 7% observation with 19% raises the mean to 3%, while the middle observations and median remain unchanged. The mean responds to every value; the median is less sensitive to this isolated change.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> would removing the largest observation necessarily improve the analysis? No: excluding an inconvenient result without a defensible rule can distort evidence. Investigate errors; do not delete valid observations merely to improve a summary.</p><p>Neither statistic gives a guaranteed next-month return. The arithmetic mean is a summary of individual monthly returns, not a compound growth rate. Recall Chapter 2: +20% and −20% average to 0% arithmetically but compound to −4%. Do not multiply a monthly arithmetic average by 12 and call it realised compound growth.</p>`,reflection:'Explain the mean–median difference and an outlier’s effect. State why a monthly arithmetic average is not a compound growth rate.',criteria:'I explained centre, outlier sensitivity, and the growth-rate distinction.'},
spread:{title:'How far apart are the observations?',objective:'CH4-03 · Compute sample standard deviation in percentage points and an explicitly defined interpolated quantile.',body:`<p>A mean hides variation. <strong>Sample standard deviation</strong> describes distances from the sample mean. Subtract the mean from each value, square the deviations, add them, divide by <strong>n−1</strong>, then take the square root. At least two observations are required. The n−1 convention estimates population variance under suitable sampling assumptions; it does not remove market-model uncertainty.</p><div class="formula">s = √[Σ(x − mean)² / (n − 1)]</div><p>Worked example [−2,0,2]%: mean 0%; squared deviations 4+0+4=8; sample variance 8/2=4 squared percentage points; sample SD = 2 <strong>percentage points</strong>. Using n instead gives about 1.63 and answers the population-SD calculation. Here all exercises request sample SD. SD is nonnegative and can be zero for a constant sample. It measures variation, not the direction of returns.</p><p>A <strong>quantile</strong> marks a position in sorted values. Software conventions differ, particularly for small samples. In this chapter use zero-based position <strong>h=(n−1)p</strong>, where p is between 0 and 1; interpolate between the neighbouring sorted observations. This is the inclusive convention.</p><p>Worked example [0,10,20,40]%, p=.75: h=3×.75=2.25. Start at zero-based index 2 (20), then move .25 of the distance to 40: 20+.25×20=<strong>25%</strong>. At p=.5 this gives the median. At p=0 or 1 it returns the minimum or maximum. Never silently mix conventions.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> if all observations rise by 5 percentage points, their mean and quantiles rise by 5; their SD stays unchanged because deviations from the shifted mean are unchanged.</p>`,reflection:'Show the n−1 denominator, identify SD units, and explain your interpolated position before comparing a software result.',criteria:'I used sample SD, percentage-point units, sorted data and the stated quantile convention.'},
correlation:{title:'Keep the pairs together.',objective:'CH4-04 · Interpret Pearson correlation with aligned observations, identify undefined cases, and reject causal or predictive overclaims.',body:`<p><strong>Pearson correlation</strong> summarises a linear association between two series of paired observations. It lies between −1 and +1 when defined. Match each asset’s return for the same month before calculating. Sorting each asset separately destroys those pairs and can manufacture a relationship.</p><div class="formula">r = Σ[(x − mean x)(y − mean y)]<br>÷ √[Σ(x − mean x)² × Σ(y − mean y)²]</div><p>Worked pairs x=[−1,0,1], y=[−2,0,2] have means 0, cross-products summing to 4, and squared sums 2 and 8. r=4/√16=<strong>1</strong>: an exact positive straight line. Changing y to [2,0,−2] gives −1. Correlation has no percentage units; it describes association rather than scale.</p><p>If x or y is constant, its squared-deviation sum is zero: the denominator vanishes and correlation is <strong>undefined</strong>, not zero. A defined correlation of zero means no measured linear association in that sample; a curved association may still exist.</p><p>Even a large correlation does not prove one asset causes the other to move. Shared influences, chance and a tiny sample can produce associations. Past correlation can change, and positive correlation does not promise future joint gains. Our six invented months cannot establish a durable diversification relationship.</p><p><strong>Guided pairing:</strong> line up 2025-01 A=−3 with 2025-01 B=−2. Continue month by month in the table below. If B’s March is missing, decide an explicit matching-date rule before analysis; do not silently pair it with April.</p>`,reflection:'Describe the date matching rule, what zero variance means, and why correlation does not establish causation or a stable future relationship.',criteria:'I preserved matching dates and rejected causal and guaranteed-future interpretations.'}
};
